## Observed (2026-08-31, live incident)

x-alpha hit 100% disk (0 bytes free). A lane's 'just rtest' run there exited 0 while the underlying remote cargo build had FAILED with 'No space left on device'. Any automation trusting the exit code reports a false green. The SUITE-VERIFIED line protocol is the existing guard (a run without the line is not a pass), but the exit code contradicting the actual outcome defeats scripted gating and invites human error.

## Expected

scripts/remote-build.py must propagate the remote command's real exit status through every path (build failure, rsync failure, ssh drop, ENOSPC mid-compile). A failed remote step can never surface as exit 0 locally.

## Notes

- Incident context: freed ~9GB on x-alpha (stale b090/nav remote trees); box remains ~99% full — ~/src (170G) and ~/.cargo (91G) are the consumers, flagged to Max.
- Consider a cheap pre-flight in remote-build.py: check free space on the remote target filesystem and fail loudly with a clear message when below a floor (e.g. 10G), naming the host and suggesting the LSP_REMOTE_HOST=x-tuf override.
- Related incomplete-run hazard family: B078, B085, B092 (cargo stops at first failing binary). This is the same class — a green signal that does not mean what it says — but at the transport layer.
- Repro shape: fill the remote target fs (or a quota'd test dir), run just rtest, observe local exit 0 + failed build output.
- x-tuf works as fallback (LSP_REMOTE_HOST=x-tuf; just is now installed there).

## Peer intel (claude-mortar-vicar-eqog, 2026-08-31)
- Their four x-alpha gates in the ENOSPC window (20:00-20:35) are believed real: each SUITE-VERIFIED total reconciled arithmetically against the prior gate (+14 B095, +4 B098, +3 B102); re-verifying master on x-tuf anyway.
- Sibling false-green fixed same day in 586d18b: 'just rtest -- --test <name>' sends --test to libtest (name substring, matches nothing) -> 30/30 coverage, 0 failures, exit 0, 'SUITE-VERIFIED: 0 passed'. Mechanism: completeness measured per-binary, totals per-test — different axes. Detection now uses libtest's own 'filtered out' count.
- Their hypothesis for THIS bug: the gate reconciles what cargo PRINTED but never checks cargo actually ran; test the specific input 'ENOSPC run emits partial sections that still reconcile' — that is the case that slips through. If no sections at all are emitted the marker is withheld, so the partial-sections case is the dangerous one.
- docs/verification-hazards.md has a section on this family ('coverage and totals on different axes'); the fix for this ticket should add the ENOSPC/transport case there.